<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#hanyu-pinyin">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hanyu Pinyin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Pinyin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hanyu Pinyin is the official system for writing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-chinese">Standard Mandarin</a> Chinese in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet, adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1958 and developed by a committee that included the linguist Zhou Youguang. It is used to teach pronunciation and to type Chinese characters, became an international standard in 1982, and has replaced older systems such as Wade–Giles for spelling Chinese names worldwide.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hanyu-pinyin.jpg" alt="Hanyu Pinyin" width="320" height="142" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vmenkov · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dajia-shuo-Putonghua-2817.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matteo-ricci">Matteo Ricci</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary, wrote the first book to use the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet for Chinese, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in 1605, and Nicolas Trigault published another in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> twenty years later. Neither influenced the Chinese literati, and both were useful chiefly to Westerners. Thomas Wade produced the Wade–Giles system in 1859, and Herbert Giles improved it in his Chinese–English Dictionary of 1892; English-language publications outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> used it until 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Hanyu means the Han language, that is, Chinese, and pinyin means spelled sounds. Each syllable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-chinese">Standard Chinese</a> is spelled as an optional initial and a final, each written with one or more letters, and diacritics mark the four tones, though they are often left out when Chinese names are spelled in non-Chinese texts.</p><p>A group of mostly Chinese linguists developed it in the 1950s, including Wang Li, Lu Zhiwei, Li Jinxi, Luo Changpei and Zhou Youguang, who has been called the father of pinyin. Zhou was an economist by profession, and Premier Zhou Enlai gave him the task after the Committee for the Reform of the Chinese Written Language was created in 1955. The group drew on Gwoyeu Romatzyh of 1928, Latinxua Sin Wenz of 1931 and the diacritics of bopomofo; Zhou later said he was the son of pinyin, the result of a long tradition from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. A first draft was written in January 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The first edition was approved and adopted at the Fifth Session of the 1st <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-peoples-congress">National People's Congress</a> on 11 February 1958. It was introduced in primary schools to teach pronunciation and used to raise adult literacy, but it did not become widely used until after the Cultural Revolution; by the 1980s students learned it from an early age. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, using pinyin rather than Wade–Giles outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was seen as a political statement. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and China established diplomatic relations in 1979, Western publications on mainland China began in the early 1980s to use it.</p><p>The International Organization for Standardization published it as ISO 7098 in 1982, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> began using it in 1986. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s National Common Language Law of 2001 gave it a legal basis, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> adopted it as its official romanization in 2009. Computer input methods use it, and some Chinese dictionaries arrange their entries by it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pinyin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hanyu Pinyin</b>, the romanisation now used worldwide to spell Chinese names, was adopted by the People’s Republic in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
